  "summary": "New preprints extend DNA/RNA foundation models to niche genomes, methylation, ncRNA and RNA-RNA interactions, and test what post-training actually learns.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the maturation of sequence foundation models for DNA and RNA from generic pretraining toward domain-specific inputs, interpretability, and design. Several preprints narrow pretraining scope or enrich inputs. An apicomplexan model pretrained on 455 assemblies improves regulatory prediction in data-sparse Plasmodium. ModBERT incorporates population variants and long-read CpG methylation. B-RANKO applies a bidirectional decoder-only architecture to ncRNA. MARRI encodes opening penalties and hybridization gains for RNA-RNA interactions. Other work probes what these models capture. Region-aware pooling of mRNA embeddings beats global mean pooling. BPNet models trained on ~2,000 ENCODE TF profiles attribute negligible genome-wide binding contribution to motif cooperativity. LAMP links failed personal-genome fine-tuning to distal effects. Fine-tuned Borzoi and AlphaGenome only match variant-based expression prediction in admixed Latino individuals. On the design side, GENECraft (training-free, language model plus functional oracle) and REGAL (reinforcement learning over regulatory grammar) generate regulatory DNA. REGAL's tissue-specific enhancers were validated across species.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
